Maya Patel: FYI I'm seeing the SSE client crash intermittently when a token boundary splits a multibyte utf8 sequence. parser throws -> backlog grows -> backpressure spikes. Repro attached in the gist: https://gist.github.com/redwood/repro-sse-split-utf8
Samir Chen: ugh. can you paste the read loop you're using? maybe decoder boundaries?
Maya Patel: here's the loop we have in node (simplified):
```js
const decoder = new TextDecoder();
stream.on('data', chunk => {
  buffer += decoder.decode(chunk, { stream: true });
  // naive: try JSON.parse on buffer when we see '}\n'
  if (buffer.includes('}\n')) {
    const parts = buffer.split('\n');
    parts.forEach(p => { try { handle(JSON.parse(p)); } catch (e) { /* crash */ } });
    buffer = '';// oops
  }
});
```
Maya Patel: basic issue: split utf8 -> decoder.decode throws sometimes or returns malformed string -> JSON.parse fails and we drop buffer -> leads to leak/backpressure.
Samir Chen: yeah two issues: (1) use decoder with stream:true but only decode when you have a full codepoint boundary OR let decoder handle streaming and keep the returned string; (2) don't blow away buffer on parse error. Instead keep partial until you see valid delim.
Samir Chen: quick sketch: keep 'leftover' as Uint8Array concat of raw bytes, feed to TextDecoder.decode with stream:true, but only slice by newline delimiter detected in decoded string. on JSON.parse error, keep the bytes until you get closing bracket that yields valid parse.
Samir Chen: also add a tiny unit test that simulates chunk boundaries mid-codepoint.
Tzahi Katz: server-side we can also avoid sending chunks that split utf8 if we control the emitter. but that's brittle across transports. my preference: make client resilient + add small backoff so the client stops acking if parser backlog grows > N.
Leo Vargas: I pushed a small PR with a test harness and the safer buffer-handling: https://github.com/redwood/inference/compare/sse-buffer-safety. PTAL @Samir @Maya :eyes:
Maya Patel: looking. main thing: ensure we don't accumulate unbounded when parse keeps failing. maybe track bytesBuffered and drop oldest events if > 1MB with metric and alert.
Samir Chen: agreed. i'll add a metric and a circuit-breaker that pauses the stream and triggers a reconnect after 200ms exponential backoff. Also will add a fuzz test that randomly splits utf8.
